<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!-- WreckVerminNest_ShipChunk.xml         RimUtinni · authored 2026-09-12

     WRECKAGE_VERMIN_SPAWN_1. The wreck-def wiring half of the mechanism —
     mandrake.rm.shipvermin (src/RimMandrake/ShipVermin) ships the generic
     "nest under the wreckage" engine (RM_CompProperties_VerminNest) and
     deliberately does not decide which ThingDef counts as wreckage. This
     campaign's own call: ShipChunk_Mech, the "mechanoid ship chunk"
     (Odyssey DLC, ParentName ShipChunkBase, RimSage-verified) — the exact
     prop scattered six-per-hulk in Defs/PrefabDefs/JawaGroundHulk.xml
     ("Torn hull plate, scattered through the banks") and the same def any
     other Gravship crash/wreck event on this campaign's maps places. This
     is "ship vermin nests under the larger hulls, living on what falls"
     (design/Jawa/worldbuilding/biomes/fall_line.md:144) made literal:
     wherever a hull chunk lands, a nest can start under it.

     NOT AncientCryptosleepCasket, the hulk's OTHER prop: that def is a
     generic Core Ancient-Danger casket that spawns in ancient shrines and
     tombs across the whole game, unrelated to wreckage — patching a vermin
     nest onto every casket in every biome would not be "wreck-anchored",
     it would be everywhere.

     Gate, matching this file's siblings (JawaGroundHulk_Register.xml):
       OUTER MayRequire tests the wiring's own two ingredients are both
       active — mandrake.rm.shipvermin (owns RM_CompProperties_VerminNest)
       and Ludeon.RimWorld.Odyssey (owns ShipChunk_Mech). If either is
       absent this whole Operation is dropped at load and nothing dangles.
       INNER PatchOperationConditional tests the def itself exists before
       touching its <comps>, same discipline as every other patch in this
       folder — defensive against Odyssey ever renaming or dropping it.

     CORRECTED 2026-09-12 (live quicktest, FOUNDRY): the original comment here
     claimed ShipChunk_Mech "already ships its own <comps>" and needed no
     check-or-create dance. That was wrong — `CompProperties_InspectString`
     is real but INHERITED from ShipChunkBase; RimSage's get_def_details
     showed the resolved/post-inheritance comps list, not ShipChunk_Mech's
     own raw XML node (confirmed by reading Defs/Odyssey/ThingDefs_Buildings/
     Buildings_Gravship.xml directly: ShipChunk_Mech's own ThingDef entry has
     no literal <comps> element at all). PatchOperationAdd targets the RAW
     per-def XML tree, not the inherited one, so `.../comps` resolved to
     nothing and the Add failed silently-then-loudly (`Patch operation ...
     failed` in Player.log) on every load — this patch never actually
     attached the comp. Fixed with the same check-or-create dance
     RSW_Mynock_ShipVermin.xml already uses.

     SECOND BUG found the same session: attaching the comp was not enough
     — `ShipChunk_Mech` inherits `tickerType` from `ShipChunkBase` ->
     `BuildingBase`, and NEITHER declares one (confirmed by reading both
     Defs/Core/ThingDefs_Buildings/Buildings_Exotic.xml and
     Buildings_Base.xml directly), so it resolves to the C# default
     `TickerType.Never` (`Verse.TickerType`, value 0). A comp's `CompTick`
     is only ever invoked while its parent Thing is on a tick list, and
     `Never` means it is on none — `RM_CompVerminNest.CompTick()` would
     never fire even with the comp correctly attached. Confirmed live: 0
     spawns after stepping past the full 240,000-tick (2-4 day) window,
     with the comp verified present the whole time. Fixed by also setting
     `tickerType` to `Normal` on `ShipChunk_Mech` specifically (not
     `ShipChunkBase`, which the vanilla non-mechanoid `ShipChunk` also
     uses and has no reason to tick).

     Tuning: left at RM_CompProperties_VerminNest's own defaults (2-4 day
     interval, radius 4, group tag "ShipVermin", hard cap 12) — the SAME
     pool RSW_Mynock's breeder already presses against
     (Patches/RSW_Mynock_ShipVermin.xml in mandrake.rm.shipvermin), so a
     nest and a breeding population share one ceiling rather than stacking
     two. Retune here if a wreck nest alone should behave differently from
     that default; the field names and this file are where that lives, not
     the RimMandrake engine.
-->
<Patch>

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional" MayRequire="mandrake.rm.shipvermin,Ludeon.RimWorld.Odyssey">
    <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="ShipChunk_Mech"]</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationSequence">
      <operations>

        <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="ShipChunk_Mech"]/comps</xpath>
          <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
            <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="ShipChunk_Mech"]</xpath>
            <value>
              <comps />
            </value>
          </nomatch>
        </li>

        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="ShipChunk_Mech"]/comps</xpath>
          <value>
            <li Class="RimMandrake.ShipVermin.RM_CompProperties_VerminNest">
              <nestSpawnIntervalDays>2~4</nestSpawnIntervalDays>
              <nestSpawnRadius>4</nestSpawnRadius>
              <populationGroupTag>ShipVermin</populationGroupTag>
              <populationHardCap>12</populationHardCap>
            </li>
          </value>
        </li>

        <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="ShipChunk_Mech"]/tickerType</xpath>
          <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
            <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[defName="ShipChunk_Mech"]</xpath>
            <value>
              <tickerType>Normal</tickerType>
            </value>
          </nomatch>
        </li>

      </operations>
    </match>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
